      "worked": "Since $x^2+4>0$ for every real $x$,\n$$\\begin{align*}y(x^2+4)&=x\\\\yx^2-x+4y&=0.\\end{align*}$$\nFor $y\\ne0$, this is a quadratic in $x$. Real solutions require $\\Delta\\ge0$.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\Delta&=(-1)^2-4(y)(4y)\\\\\\Delta&=1-16y^2.\\end{align*}$$\nHence,\n$$\\begin{align*}1-16y^2&\\ge0\\\\16y^2&\\le1\\\\-\\frac14\\le y&\\le\\frac14.\\end{align*}$$\nWhen $y=0$, the equation becomes $-x=0$, giving $x=0$, so this value is included. The endpoints are attained: $x=-2$ gives $y=-\\frac14$, and $x=2$ gives $y=\\frac14$.\nThe range is $\\boxed{\\left[-\\frac14,\\frac14\\right]}$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
